    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nFord Transit Connect Recalled Again Over Rearview Cameras That Still Don't Work\n6 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nThe Ford Motor Company is pulling 506 vans back into dealerships because the rearview cameras installed under a previous campaign do not do the job as intended. As it happens, the replacement cameras are an older revision that never contained the fix at all.\nDuring the launch of the remedy for safety recall 25V572, a transcription error listed the recalled camera assembly as the remedy part. The dealer bulletin should have said the fix wasn't available yet. Given the aforementioned, service centers ordered what the paperwork named rather than rearview cameras that actually work as intended.\nThe camera itself is produced by Magna, and there are a few problems with it. From distorted to inverted images and intermittent to persistent loss of image, said design does not meet the federal requirements for rearview cameras. The safety risk that justified the original campaign is still there, unaddressed, in all 506 vans.\nSaid US-market population comes from warranty claims on vans repaired under the previous recall. FoMoCo identified the problem reasonably fast. The Critical Concerns Review Group started investigating the matter in June 2026, with engineers spending the summer chasing down which vans had been serviced under the wrong instructions.\nBy September 21, they had the final tally for the United States market. The safety boffins further confirmed no accidents or injuries potentially related to the recall condition. As per the recall documentation, the driver gets no warning before the image misbehaves.\nPhoto: Ford Production dates for the subject vehicles range from August 1, 2014, through July 28, 2015. Despite being North American vans, they were sourced from the Valencia plant in Spain rather than North America. The Ford Motor Company decided on Spain because the Transit Connect was originally designed for Europe, where compact vans are highly popular.\nFord claims that affected VINs became searchable on the NHTSA's portal back on October 7, 2026. The fix is understandably free, and just like before, a camera swap is on the menu. On this occasion, however, the replacement assemblies will be produced under an updated process with a greater focus on quality control.\nCustomers who already paid a Ford service center out of pocket have a route back too, since FoMoCo runs a general reimbursement plan for safety recall work. Remedy letters are planned to land no later than December 17, 2027, which indicates that replacement cameras are not readily available at dealers nationwide.\nThe Ford Motor Company ended sales of the Transit Connect in North America after the 2023 model year, citing poor demand for compact and small vans in this part of the world. Had sales been a little better, Ford may have gone through with a next-gen model that was expected to be produced in Mexico.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFord Ford Transit Connect US van recall\nDownload: Ford Transit Connect Blank or Distorted Rearview Camera Image Recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 5 Most Iconic Muscle Cars Introduced During the 1965 Model Year",
